<h2 id="section-1">Describe the tilt before trying to correct it</h2>
<p>Separate three observations. The front may lean away from the ear, the entire earring may rotate sideways, or a hanging element may turn independently while its base stays still. Those are different questions for a seller. Photograph the comfortable, resting arrangement from the front and side without pulling the ear into a different position. If the jewelry is painful or the ear looks newly changed, do not keep wearing it to obtain a better picture.</p>
<p>Record whether the behavior was present from the first use, appeared after changing a component, or followed an impact. That sequence is useful even when the explanation remains uncertain. Do not label the problem “weak ears” or assume a more expensive material will fix it. Material disclosure matters, but it does not replace information about geometry and closure fit.</p>
<h2 id="section-4">Use a component map</h2>
<div class="sj-table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Comparison / Comparaison"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Observation</th><th scope="col">Information to request</th><th scope="col">What it does not establish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Front leans forward at rest</td><td>Side profile, front projection and intended back</td><td>That a bigger back will solve every case.</td></tr><tr><td>Back cannot sit as intended</td><td>Instructions and compatibility for the exact parts</td><td>That extra tightening is appropriate.</td></tr><tr><td>Ornament rotates on its connection</td><td>Normal range of movement and orientation</td><td>That every moving element is defective.</td></tr><tr><td>Behavior changed after a knock</td><td>Inspection or repair guidance</td><td>That bending it back at home is safe.</td></tr><tr><td>Left and right look different</td><td>Separate observations for each ear</td><td>That either ear has a medical condition.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>Look at the arrangement as a set of relationships. A front that projects away from the post can feel different from a shape whose mass stays nearer the ear. That is a reason to request a side image, not a formula for calculating an individual comfort threshold. Avoid choosing a replacement from the diameter of the backing alone.</p>
<h2 id="section-7">Change the purchase question</h2>
<p>Instead of asking “Will these stay straight?”, ask the seller to show the complete earring from the side, identify the supplied back, and explain whether the ornament is meant to move. If you are comparing two versions, request the measurements and weight of each complete version. A base photographed without its decorative element cannot answer a question about the finished arrangement.</p>
<p>In a fictional comparison, two earrings have the same listed weight. One carries a broad front close to the ear; the other has a projecting ornament. Your next step is not to declare one comfortable and the other uncomfortable. It is to establish their profiles and intended fitting, and compare them with a piece you already wear comfortably. Keep any on-ear photograph at a similar viewing distance; close-up perspective can make a small projection look dramatic.</p>
<h2 id="section-10">When a repair question is the better route</h2>
<p>If a post appears bent, a latch has changed or parts no longer engage normally, stop treating the problem as styling. Contact the maker or a competent jeweler. Do not add glue, squeeze precision parts with household pliers or rely on a closure that needs repeated improvisation. A repair assessment can also establish whether a part should be replaced rather than adjusted.</p>
<p>An adhesive support or a larger back may be mentioned in community discussions. Treat that as someone’s experience, not evidence that it is suitable for your ear, skin or earring. A temporary visual correction can conceal a different fit problem. You do not need to make every pair work; deciding to return an unsuitable piece under the seller’s actual conditions can be the practical outcome.</p>
<h2 id="section-13">Supporting checks for this decision</h2>
<p>The examples below are comparison tools, not SUIJI product prices, certifications or test results. Apply the questions to the actual reference you are considering.</p>
<h3 id="section-15">First ask: weight per earring or per pair?</h3>
<p>A listing that says “8 g” may mean one earring, the pair, or a product plus a removable component. Those readings are not interchangeable. A useful product page labels both the unit and the configuration:</p>
<p>The values must come from production-representative samples. A CAD estimate, render, or different prototype should not be presented as the delivered product’s measured weight.</p>
<h3 id="section-18">Why centre of gravity matters</h3>
<p>Weight farther from the piercing creates more leverage than the same weight close to the lobe. That is why a long pendant and a compact hoop with an identical scale reading may feel different. Movement, backing shape and the direction in which the design pulls also matter.</p>
<p>A simple diagram can mark the attachment point, the approximate mass zone and the expected direction of movement. This is an explanatory aid, not a clinical comfort test. It helps a shopper understand why a long shape may swing or why a front-heavy stud may tilt.</p>
<h3 id="section-21">There is no universal comfortable gram number</h3>
<p>Lobe anatomy, the placement and age of a healed piercing, duration, closure, activity and personal experience all change tolerance. A universal threshold without context creates false precision.</p>
<p>Use three kinds of information instead:</p>
<ol><li><strong>Measured fact:</strong> weight and dimensions of the exact configuration.</li><li><strong>Comparable reference:</strong> an honest side-by-side with a familiar model or object at the same scale.</li><li><strong>Wear observation:</strong> a documented test duration, configuration and number of testers, described as an observation rather than a promise.</li></ol>
<h3 id="section-25">How to compare two listings in three minutes</h3>
<p>Create a small comparison note before checkout:</p>
<div class="sj-table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Comparison / Comparaison"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Question</th><th scope="col">Listing A</th><th scope="col">Listing B</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Weight is per ear?</td><td>yes/no/unclear</td><td>yes/no/unclear</td></tr><tr><td>Exact worn configuration named?</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Height × width × depth in mm?</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Front and side wearing views?</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Closure and post visible?</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Return/hygiene conditions readable?</td><td></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>“Unclear” is meaningful. Ask the brand rather than assuming. Save the reply if the measurement influences the purchase.</p>
<h3 id="section-29">Three contexts where the same weight leads to different choices</h3>
<p>For a <strong>desk day</strong>, phone, headset and backing pressure may matter more than swing. For an <strong>evening event</strong>, length, dancing, greetings and the final hairstyle change the test. For a <strong>winter commute</strong>, scarf and coat contact can outweigh the scale number. Try the complete context rather than interpreting grams alone.</p>
<p>When a seller gives a tolerance, ask what was measured and whether the figure describes your complete configuration. More decimal places do not necessarily mean better information. A measured result, an estimate and a range across several pieces are different descriptions. Keep the answer with the reference so you can compare another version without transferring a measurement that does not belong to it.</p>
<h2 id="section-32">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3 id="section-33">Does forward tilt mean an earring is too heavy?</h3>
<p>It can be one observation in a broader fit question, but the weight number alone cannot establish the cause. Consider projection, components and how the piece sits, and seek advice when the behavior changes.</p>
<h3 id="section-35">Should I tighten the backing until the front is upright?</h3>
<p>Do not use compression as a visual correction. Follow the product’s normal fitting instructions. If the intended fit does not work for you, a different configuration is more useful than forcing the result.</p>
<h3 id="section-37">Is a moving charm supposed to face forward all the time?</h3>
<p>Ask about that exact design. Some elements move independently; others have a defined orientation. A still product photograph does not show what happens as you turn your head or walk.</p>
<h3 id="section-39">Can SUIJI confirm every combination from a photo?</h3>
<p>A photo can begin a product question, but it does not establish universal compatibility, dimensions or personal fit. Keep the exact base and ornament references available when asking about a combination.</p>
